#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:71
msgid "Content Security Policy. A rule that tells the browser which sources a page may load scripts, styles and images from. It blocks injected code, but a rule that is too strict also blocks things you need."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:72
msgid "A header that tells browsers to open your site only over HTTPS for a set time. Once a browser remembers it, there is no way back to plain HTTP until the time runs out."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:73
msgid "A trial mode of a security rule: the browser does not block anything, it only reports what it would have blocked. Use it to see the effect before you enforce the rule."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:74
msgid "A small request that the WordPress editor and dashboard send to the server every 15 to 60 seconds. It keeps the login alive and warns about simultaneous editing, but on a busy site it adds server load."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:75
msgid "An old WordPress interface (xmlrpc.php) for remote apps. Few sites need it today, and attackers like it for password guessing, so it is usually safe to turn off."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:76
msgid "The interface through which apps, the block editor and plugins read and write site data over web addresses starting with /wp-json/."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:77
msgid "The WordPress scheduler. It runs timed jobs such as publishing scheduled posts, checking updates or sending e-mails, and it wakes up when someone opens a page."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:78
msgid "Time to live: how many seconds a copy of the data (a DNS answer, a cached page) may be reused before it is fetched again. A short TTL means changes show up faster, a long one means fewer requests."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:79
msgid "The address book of the internet. It turns a domain name into the server that serves it and says where the e-mail of the domain goes."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:80
msgid "A DNS record that lists the servers allowed to send e-mail for your domain, so receivers can spot forged messages."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:81
msgid "A digital signature added to every e-mail you send. The receiver checks it against a key in your DNS to be sure the message is yours and was not changed on the way."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:82
msgid "A DNS record that tells receivers what to do with e-mail that fails the SPF and DKIM checks (nothing, spam folder, reject) and where to send reports."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:83
msgid "A way to let one service act on your account at another service (for example read your Google reviews) without giving it your password. You approve the access once and can withdraw it later."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:84
msgid "A web address that another service calls the moment something happens, for example when a form is sent, so it can react without asking again and again."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:85
msgid "Regular expression. A compact pattern for matching text, such as every address that starts with /blog/ followed by digits. Powerful, but a small mistake can match far more than you meant."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:86
msgid "A permanent redirect. The old address is gone for good, visitors and search engines move to the new one, and the ranking of the old page is passed on."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:87
msgid "A temporary redirect. The old address will come back, so search engines keep it in their index and do not move the ranking."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:88
msgid "Gone. The page was removed on purpose and will not return, which makes search engines drop it faster than an ordinary \"not found\" (404)."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:89
msgid "The one address a page wants search engines to count when the same content can be reached under several addresses (with parameters, with or without a slash)."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:90
msgid "The part of a web address that names a page, for example \"about-us\" in example.com/about-us/."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:91
msgid "A piece of data that WordPress stores temporarily, with an expiry time, so it does not have to be computed again on every request."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:92
msgid "A fast memory (usually Redis or Memcached) where WordPress keeps results of database queries, so pages build faster. It needs a server that provides it."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:93
msgid "Images and frames further down the page are loaded only when the visitor scrolls near them. The first screen opens faster and less data is transferred."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:94
msgid "A modern image format that is usually 25 to 35 percent smaller than JPEG at the same quality. All current browsers show it."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:95
msgid "A newer image format, often smaller than WebP at the same quality. Most current browsers show it, but it takes longer to create."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:96
msgid "Largest Contentful Paint. How long it takes until the biggest thing on the first screen (usually a photo or a heading) is visible. Google counts under 2.5 seconds as good."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:97
msgid "Cumulative Layout Shift. How much the page jumps while it loads, for example when an image appears and pushes the text down. Lower is better, under 0.1 is good."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:98
msgid "Content delivery network. A set of servers around the world that keep copies of your images, styles and scripts and serve them from the place closest to the visitor."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:99
msgid "A form field that real visitors never see. A bot fills in every field it finds, so a filled honeypot tells the form that the sender is not a person."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:100
msgid "A small puzzle that the visitor's browser solves by itself in a fraction of a second. It costs a single visitor nothing, but a bot that sends thousands of requests has to solve thousands of puzzles."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:101
msgid "Finding out the login names of a site, for example through /?author=1 or the REST API. A known login name makes password guessing easier."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:102
msgid "Separate passwords that WordPress creates for apps and scripts that connect to your site. Each can be revoked on its own without changing your login password."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:103
msgid "Single sign-on. You log in once, for example with Google, and are let into other services without typing a password again."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:104
msgid "Model Context Protocol. A standard way for AI assistants to use tools and data of another program, here of your site, in a controlled way."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:105
msgid "Retrieval-augmented generation. The AI first looks up the matching parts of your own content and answers from them, instead of guessing from general knowledge."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:106
msgid "A plain text file in the root of the site that gives AI assistants a short, tidy overview of what the site is about and which pages matter."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:107
msgid "A block of structured data in the page code that tells search engines exactly what the page is (an article, a product, an event) without changing how it looks."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:108
msgid "The shared vocabulary of structured data that search engines understand. It lets a result show stars, prices or opening hours."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:109
msgid "A pair of keys that identifies your site to the browser push services, so that only your site can send push notifications to the people who subscribed."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:110
msgid "A short way to write a whole range of IP addresses, for example 192.168.1.0/24 stands for 256 addresses starting at 192.168.1.0."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:111
msgid "The text with which a browser or a bot introduces itself to the server, for example the name of the browser and its version."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:112
msgid "A cap on how many requests one visitor or address may send in a given time. Anything above it is refused, which slows down bots and guessing of passwords."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:113
msgid "A one-time token that WordPress adds to forms and requests to prove that they really came from your own screen and not from a forged link."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:114
msgid "The standard way to send e-mail through a mail server with a login. Sending through a proper mail server lands in the inbox far more often than the default PHP mail."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:115
msgid "The standard way to read e-mail from a mail server while the messages stay on the server. Used when the site needs to look into a mailbox."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:116
msgid "A quick check that tells a person from a script, for example a puzzle or a tick box. It stops mass attempts but not every clever script."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:117
msgid "A public number for a known security hole in software, so everybody means the same flaw when they talk about it."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:118
msgid "The opposite lookup of DNS: from an IP address back to a name. Real search engine bots have a name of their own company, so this unmasks bots that only pretend."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:119
msgid "The small pieces of text, roughly three quarters of a word each, that an AI reads and writes. Providers bill and limit by tokens, so longer questions and answers cost more."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:120
msgid "The standing briefing an AI gets before every conversation: who it is, what it may talk about and how it should sound. Visitors never see it."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:121
msgid "A simple text format for structured data: one fact per line, written as name, colon, value. Here it holds the title, address and date of a page at the top of the markdown."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:122
msgid "The browser guesses which page the visitor will open next and starts loading it in the background, so the click feels instant. It costs some extra traffic for guesses that turn out wrong."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:123
msgid "Downloads the files of a page in advance but does not run anything yet. It is cheap and safe."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:124
msgid "Loads and builds the whole next page in the background, so it appears at once when opened. It is faster than prefetch but also more demanding, and the page may count a visit that never happens."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:125
msgid "The moment when a visitor seems about to leave the page, for example by moving the mouse towards the browser bar or by switching the tab."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:126
msgid "Google Analytics 4. The current version of the Google service that counts visitors and what they do on the site."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:127
msgid "A header that tells browsers and search engines when to come back. During maintenance it says that the pause is temporary, so search engines do not drop your pages."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:128
msgid "A signature computed from the message and a secret key. Anybody who knows the key can check that the message is genuine and unchanged; without the key it cannot be forged."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:129
msgid "A plugin in the special folder wp-content/mu-plugins. WordPress loads it always, it cannot be turned off in the plugin list and it runs before the normal plugins."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:130
msgid "Extra data saved with a post, page or product, such as a custom field. Plugins keep their own settings of each item there."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:131
msgid "A way to group content, for example categories and tags. Plugins can add their own, such as product categories."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:132
msgid "Older saved versions of a post that WordPress keeps so you can go back. They fill the database over time."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:133
msgid "A setting in wp-config.php that makes WordPress start scheduled tasks by redirecting the visitor once. It helps on servers where the normal way does not work, but it adds a parameter to the address."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:134
msgid "A setting in wp-config.php that stops WordPress from starting scheduled tasks when a page opens. Then the server has to start them regularly, otherwise nothing scheduled runs."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Core/Glossary.php:135
msgid "The European data protection law. It says what you may collect about visitors, that you must tell them and that they can ask you to delete it."
msgstr ""

