#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:12
msgid "Chooses the service that delivers every e-mail the site sends: orders, password resets, form messages. Your own mail server ([[smtp|SMTP]]) or one of the e-mail services with an API."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:13
msgid "Mail sent through a proper service is far less likely to land in spam than the plain mail of the web server."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:14
msgid "If the chosen service is not set up or its login is wrong, sending fails and password resets and order e-mails do not arrive. Send a test e-mail after every change, and set a fallback connection."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:17
msgid "A second service that takes over when sending with the first one fails or is not set up. Both attempts are written to the log."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:18
msgid "An outage of one service no longer means lost e-mails."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:19
msgid "The fallback must also have its sender verified. A failed message may be delivered by the second service, so the recipient sees a different sending server."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:20
msgid "Leave it on \"None\" if you have only one service."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:23
msgid "The sender address recipients see on every e-mail. Empty keeps what WordPress or the plugin chooses, usually wordpress@yourdomain."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:24
msgid "Use an address on your own domain, such as info@yourdomain.com, so recipients recognize you."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:25
msgid "Most services refuse or mark as spam an address they have not verified, and an address on a domain without proper [[spf|SPF]] and [[dkim|DKIM]] records is often filtered. Verify the address or the domain with your service first."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:28
msgid "Replaces the sender address of every message with the one above, even when a plugin sets its own. Without it the address is replaced only when the plugin leaves the WordPress default."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:29
msgid "All mail comes from one address your service accepts."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:30
msgid "Contact forms that put the visitor's address in the sender field lose it, so \"Reply\" no longer goes to the visitor unless the form sets a Reply-To header."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:33
msgid "The sender name recipients see next to the address, for example the name of your company. Empty keeps what WordPress or the plugin chooses."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:34
msgid "A familiar name is opened more often and looks less like spam."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:37
msgid "Replaces the sender name of every message with the one above, even when a plugin sets its own."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:38
msgid "All mail has the same sender name."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:39
msgid "A form plugin that puts the visitor's name there loses it."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:42
msgid "The address of your mail server, which your mail provider gives you, for example smtp.example.com. It is used by [[smtp|SMTP]]."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:43
msgid "The site hands every e-mail to this server for delivery."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:44
msgid "A typo makes sending fail. Check it with the test e-mail below."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:47
msgid "The door of the mail server the site connects to. It must match the encryption: 587 with TLS is the usual choice, 465 with SSL is the older one, 25 is often blocked by hosts."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:48
msgid "The right port and encryption together keep the login and the messages private on their way."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:49
msgid "A port that does not fit the encryption makes the connection hang or fail. Many hosts block port 25."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:52
msgid "How the connection to the mail server is protected. TLS (usually port 587) and SSL (usually port 465) encrypt it; None sends everything in plain text."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:53
msgid "With encryption, nobody on the way can read your login or your messages."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:54
msgid "With None, the password travels in plain text. Use None only for a server on the same machine."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:57
msgid "The login name for the mail server. Often it is the full e-mail address, but with some providers it is a special name."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:58
msgid "The server uses it with the password to accept your messages."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:61
msgid "The password for the mail server. It is stored encrypted and never shown again. Leave the field empty to keep the current one."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:62
msgid "Fill it only when you set it up or change it."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:63
msgid "Use a dedicated or application-specific password if your provider offers one. A changed account password makes sending fail until you update it here."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:66
msgid "The key that lets this site send through your Brevo account. Create it in Brevo under SMTP & API. It is stored encrypted and never shown again. Leave the field empty to keep the current one."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:67
msgid "Use \"Verify API key\" to check that it works."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:68
msgid "Anyone with the key can send mail in your name. Never paste it in a chat or an e-mail, and create a new one if it leaks."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:71
msgid "The ID of an [[oauth|OAuth]] client that you create in Google Cloud. It lets the site ask Google for permission to send mail from your Gmail account, without storing your Gmail password."
msgstr ""

#: includes/Modules/SmtpEmail/help.php:72
msgid "After you save it together with the secret, the \"Connect Gmail account\" button opens the Google consent screen."
